description:
Found by s533d's D0 consumer comparison (1c port 2; F rows promote:$id_re legacy 0 / binder 1).

Probe (scratch/s533d/pr/R1.pl in the s533d worktree):

    my $id_re = qr/[a-z]+/;
    sub m1 { my $s = shift; $s =~ s/(${id_re}[\$\@:]\S+)/<$1>/g; $s }
    print m1("see foo\@bar and x"), "\n";
    my $d = '[a-zA-Z]:';
    sub abs1 { my $f = shift; $f =~ m{^$d[\\/]} ? 2 : 0 }
    print abs1('C:\\x'), abs1('zz'), "\n";

perl 5.40.3:            see <foo@bar> and x / 20
base 0f2db817:          see foo<@bar> and x / 00      -- SILENT WRONG (both lines)
s533d tree:             see <foo@bar> and x / 20

Mechanism: the legacy `_block_captures_name_0` read text mentions through its own per-canon patterns (`_canon_text_patterns`), whose scalar arm carried `(?![\[\{])` -- `$x[` / `${x}[` was taken for an ELEMENT of @x.  Inside a regex perl decides that with toke.c's intuit_more (a character class here), which Pl::InterpScan implements and the emission uses -- so the sub's code read the SCALAR `$x`, while the capture scan said the sub does not capture it: the file lexical was not promoted and the hoisted sub read the package cell `p-defcell $x` (nil).  Where another sub DID capture the name (File::Spec::Win32's `$DRIVE_RX`) the value was right and only the `:captures` manifest was short.

Real sites: core lib Pod/Simple/RTF.pm:248 (`${id_re}[\$\@\:_<>\(\\\*]\S+`, do_middle -- the RTF formatter's proofing-exemption pass), its copy in the board's Pod-Simple-3.47; core File/Spec/Win32.pm:121 (manifest only).

FIXED by s533d (the capture question asks Pl::Binder::captures_name, whose interpolated refs ARE Pl::InterpScan's -- one scanner for the emission and the capture verdict).  Guard: Pl/t/capture-binder-01.t rows "a file lexical as `${x}[...]` in a sub's regex".


